The == operator compares the references of the objects, not their actual values. On the other hand, the .equals() method compares the actual values of the objects.

The == operator is not always bad. It is useful when you want to check if two references point to the same object. However, when comparing objects for equality, it's generally better to use the .equals() method, as it checks the actual value of the object, which is more meaningful in most cases.

Here's an example:

```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";

// Using == operator
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("The strings are the same");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are different");
}

// Using .equals() method
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("The strings are the same");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are different");
}
```

In this example, both `str1` and `str2` are "Hello". The == operator will return `true`, while the .equals() method will return `false` because they are different objects, even though their contents are the same.

